course: dictionary
lesson: 2907
title: Downsizing and hidden meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Downsizing means making something smaller. A company may use it when it cuts jobs, and a person may use it when they move to a smaller home. The word has more than one meaning, so the sentence around it tells you which one is meant.
explain: Read this: «A company that sacks hundreds of workers may call it "downsizing", "rightsizing" or "restructuring", which is spin to make it sound sensible.» The clue is sacks hundreds of workers. Sacking means taking away someone's job, so here downsizing means cutting jobs, not moving house.
explain: Notice the word spin. A euphemism is a softer, politer word used to hide an unpleasant fact. Downsizing sounds calm and tidy, but it can hide the hard truth that people lose their jobs. Ask yourself: what is the plain fact behind this nicer word?
explain: Grandma and Grandad might downsize to a smaller flat when their children have grown up. Same word, different situation. Use the clues around it, then swap your meaning into the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
fact: Downsizing means making something smaller, such as a company, a home, or the number of things you own.
fact: Downsize is the verb: downsizes, downsized and downsizing are its other forms.
fact: When a company is downsizing, it is cutting jobs, so workers lose their jobs.
fact: Downsizing is a euphemism when it is used to soften the news that workers are being sacked.
fact: Useful phrases: downsizing of staff, and downsize to a smaller flat.
exercise: «A company that sacks hundreds of workers may call it "downsizing".» What does downsizing mean here? | Hiring hundreds of new workers | Moving the company to a bigger building | *Cutting jobs, so workers lose their jobs | Giving everyone a holiday
exercise: Grandma and Grandad will downsize to a smaller flat. What does downsize mean here? | To buy a bigger house with a large garden | *To move to a smaller home | To build an extra room onto their house
exercise: Why do companies say "downsizing" instead of "sacking workers"? | To make the news sound louder and more exciting | *To soften the hard news | To explain how to pack boxes | To say it in a different language
test: In «which is spin to make it sound sensible», what does spin mean? | *A way of presenting news so it sounds better than it really is | Spinning a coin on a table | Turning round and round quickly | Cleaning up a messy room
test: Last year the firm ___ its staff by a third. Which form fits best? | downsizes | *downsized | downsizing | downsize
exam: Why does the sentence say that downsizing "sounds sensible", and what is the plain truth behind it? | It means nothing at all | *It is a softer word that hides people losing their jobs | It means the company has too many chairs | It means the workers have become very small
